## Is atrial fibrillation a disability?

Under the **Equality Act 2010**, a condition counts as a disability if it has a **substantial** (more than minor) and **long-term** (12 months or more) effect on your normal daily activities. The law looks at how you would be **without treatment**. AF that causes frequent symptoms, such as breathlessness and tiredness, may be covered.

## Reasonable adjustments

- time off for appointments, cardioversion or ablation
- more breaks, or less heavy physical work
- flexible hours if tiredness is a problem
- a phased return after treatment

If you drive a lorry or bus for work, you must check the DVLA rules.

## Benefits

Most people with AF alone will not qualify for PIP. It may help if AF, often together with heart failure or another condition, affects activities such as:

- **moving around**, if breathlessness or dizziness stop you walking more than a short distance reliably
- **preparing food** or **washing**, if you need to stop and rest often

{% checker tool="pip" /%}

If you are over State Pension age, look at [Attendance Allowance](/benefits/attendance-allowance/) instead of PIP. It is for people who need help with personal care or supervision.

## If you cannot work

If your health means you cannot work, or can only work a little, you may be able to get [New Style Employment and Support Allowance](/benefits/esa/) or extra money in [Universal Credit](/benefits/universal-credit-health/). These are separate from PIP, and you can get them at the same time.

Free, independent advisers, such as **Citizens Advice** or a local welfare rights service, can help you check what you can claim and fill in forms.
